title: Proposition 10
---
order: 10
---
short_label: XI.10
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If two straight lines meeting one another are parallel to two straight lines meeting one another not in the same plane, then they contain equal angles.
----
proof:

Let the two straight lines {AB} and {BC} meeting one another be parallel to the two straight lines {DE} and {EF} meeting one another not in the same plane.

I say that the angle {ABC|angB} equals the angle {DEF|angE}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="310" height="300" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XI.10 diagram" src="propXI10.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // AB, BC meet at B in the upper plane P; DE, EF, parallel to them, meet
    // at E in the lower plane Q. The four are cut off equal (in the figure
    // D = E + A - B and F = E + C - B already make them so), AD, BE, CF are
    // joined and shown equal and parallel (I.33, XI.9), then AC, DF, and
    // I.8 gives the equal angles.
    //
    // Joyce draws AB, BC, AC and DE, EF, DF only as the edges of the filled
    // triangles ABC and DEF. The walk needs the four given lines before AC
    // and DF are joined, so AB, BC, DE, EF, AC, DF are solid slide twins,
    // hidden at rest (initiallyHidden); the triangles trace and fill when
    // the two sides and base are compared for I.8. The planes P and Q
    // frame every slide but are never highlighted. Angle and triangle names
    // collide (ABC, DEF): the triangles own them, the angle markers angB,
    // angE take the display override.
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): X, Y, Z (far-off direction
